# Applied by Cloudflare Pages to every static asset response (never to `npm run dev`).
#
# The Content-Security-Policy below is pinned to our specific Cloudflare Access team domain.
# This prevents any other Cloudflare Access tenant on the internet from serving scripts 
# or making network connections into this origin.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://kamenkolev.cloudflareaccess.com; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' https://kamenkolev.cloudflareaccess.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'

# `base-uri 'none'` and `form-action 'none'` are the two directives that do NOT inherit default-src,
# so they have to be written out. They are the ones that matter most here: a `<base>` injection or a
# hijacked form is the classic route from "minor DOM bug" to "the API key in localStorage is posted
# somewhere else".

# Pages does not apply this file to responses generated by Pages Functions - the rules above
# therefore never reach /api/ai-proxy. The relay carries its own `Cache-Control: no-store` and
# `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` from `shared/aiProxy.ts`, which is the only place they can be
# set. Nothing is declared here for /api/*.